WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:03.400
"This is a long term war."

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
"This is a different type of enemy 
then we’re used to."

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.507
"And we're adjusting our thinking 
to the new type of enemy."

00:00:09.507 --> 00:00:12.470
Days after the 9/11 attacks, 
President George W. Bush

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.665
launched the global war on terror.

00:00:14.665 --> 00:00:18.340
In October 2001, he sent the military 
to invade Afghanistan

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.980
and hunt down members of al-Qaeda
and its ally, the Taliban.&nbsp;

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:25.730
It also offered cash rewards to anyone 
who would help capture a terrorist.&nbsp;

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.794
Often enough money to change lives.&nbsp;

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:32.180
So, many in Afghanistan and Pakistan
took advantage of the offer

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.960
and turned hundreds of men over
often with little evidence.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.188
The US sent those men to secret prisons 
called black sites. 

00:00:39.188 --> 00:00:41.615
Where they interrogated and tortured them.

00:00:41.615 --> 00:00:44.722
But within a month, they started searching
for a larger, permanent prison 

00:00:44.722 --> 00:00:46.799
to consolidate all these prisoners.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.799 --> 00:00:51.768
Eventually settling on this old Navy base
in Guantánamo Bay, Cuba.&nbsp;

00:00:56.949 --> 00:01:00.998
In January 2002, the first detainees 
began arriving.

00:01:00.998 --> 00:01:04.750
One of them was Moath al-Alwi who spent the
next two decades in this prison

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.317
because the US government deemed him 
too dangerous to be set free.

00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:10.870
But they never charged him with a crime.&nbsp;

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.976
And he’s not alone.&nbsp;

00:01:12.976 --> 00:01:15.540
Hundreds of men were imprisoned in Guantánamo.&nbsp;

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.161
Few were charged with a crime.&nbsp;

00:01:17.161 --> 00:01:18.337
Some were tortured.&nbsp;

00:01:18.337 --> 00:01:20.479
And none received a fair trial.&nbsp;

00:01:21.741 --> 00:01:25.000
It’s a prison that's operated outside 
the bounds of law.&nbsp;

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.090
So, how did the US get away with this?

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:31.017
And why is Guantánamo Bay still open?

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.500
There are two myths about the people 
who were picked up.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.899
One is that they are all 
hardened, capable terrorists.&nbsp;

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:51.077
The other is that they were all innocent shepherds.

00:01:51.077 --> 00:01:52.549
This is Dan Fried.

00:01:52.549 --> 00:01:55.277
He worked in the US foreign service for over
40 years 

00:01:55.277 --> 00:01:57.501
and was assigned to Guantanamo in 2009.

00:01:57.501 --> 00:02:00.191
The conclusion I had is that there was 
kind of a bell curve. 

00:02:00.191 --> 00:02:05.000
At one low end, there were actually 
hardened terrorists.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.087
Like this man, Osama bin-Laden’s 
personal secretary.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.087 --> 00:02:13.684
There were others that were sometimes petty
criminals and drug dealers.

00:02:13.684 --> 00:02:17.530
Not great people, but not criminal masterminds,
not terrorists.&nbsp;

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:21.993
And the top of the bell curve were people
just a little more involved

00:02:21.993 --> 00:02:23.375
than what I just described.

00:02:23.375 --> 00:02:26.790
At the other end were people who were swept
up and shouldn't have been there at all.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.153
Like a group of Uigher men.

00:02:28.153 --> 00:02:31.942
Members of a mainly Muslim ethnic group who
were fleeing persecution in China.

00:02:31.942 --> 00:02:36.668
And they wound up in al-Qaeda camps and 
we didn't know what to do with them

00:02:36.668 --> 00:02:38.737
and so they ended up in Guantánamo.&nbsp;

00:02:38.737 --> 00:02:43.069
In fact, many detainees were put in Guantánamo
based on little evidence.&nbsp;

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:46.380
Yet Bush officials often described them all
as terrorist.&nbsp;

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.559
"I mean these are people who would gnaw through
hydraulic lines of the back of a C-17 

00:02:50.559 --> 00:02:51.769
to bring it down.”

00:02:51.769 --> 00:02:53.470
"Very, very dangerous people."

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:57.325
By 2003, there were nearly 700 
Guantánamo detainees 

00:02:57.325 --> 00:02:59.799
and virtually none of them were 
charged with a crime.&nbsp;

00:03:00.774 --> 00:03:04.127
"The American flag flies again 
over our embassy in Kabul."

00:03:04.127 --> 00:03:07.718
"Terrorists who once occupied Afghanistan

00:03:07.718 --> 00:03:10.841
now occupy cells at Guantánamo Bay."

00:03:14.416 --> 00:03:19.110
The Bush administration chose Guantánamo Bay 
largely because of its unique location.

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:23.559
It was under US control, but it wasn’t technically
inside the US.

00:03:23.559 --> 00:03:28.005
So, they claimed US law wouldn’t apply to
the detainees held here.

00:03:28.005 --> 00:03:31.386
If they put them in a US prison, 
they'd have to either charge them with a crime 

00:03:31.386 --> 00:03:34.069
and put them on trial or release them.

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:37.924
In other words, under US law, prisoners 
would have ways to get out.

00:03:37.924 --> 00:03:41.081
For bad reasons, they didn't trust 
the criminal justice system.

00:03:41.081 --> 00:03:43.038
It gives too many rights to the prisoners.

00:03:43.038 --> 00:03:46.300
There was a sense that the old rules 
had to be thrown out.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.440
The US also claimed that international law
didn’t apply to these detainees either.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.153
Even though 196 countries, including the US,
signed the Geneva Conventions:

00:03:55.153 --> 00:03:57.959
a set of laws protecting prisoners of war.

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:01.539
But for those laws to apply, 
the US would have to define the detainees 

00:04:01.539 --> 00:04:03.220
as prisoners of war.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.845
Then they couldn’t interrogate them.

00:04:04.845 --> 00:04:07.770
And would have to release them as soon 
as the conflict ends.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:12.551
We didn't want to give them those rights because
we were so fearful of a new terrorist attack.

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:14.849
We wanted to interrogate and frankly 

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:17.378
some parts of the US government 
interrogated through torture.

00:04:17.378 --> 00:04:19.603
I mean, that's—fact.

00:04:19.603 --> 00:04:23.806
So instead of charging them as criminals 
or calling them prisoners of war

00:04:23.806 --> 00:04:25.626
the US made up a new term.

00:04:25.626 --> 00:04:27.911
Unlawful enemy combatants.

00:04:27.911 --> 00:04:32.320
The US claimed it could hold “unlawful,
enemy combatants” without charges in Guantánamo,

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:33.664
indefinitely.

00:04:33.664 --> 00:04:37.004
And the detainees couldn’t challenge 
it in court.

00:04:37.004 --> 00:04:41.901
But the US could prosecute them, in a new
court system, run entirely by the military.

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:47.824
These courts were designed to be complex,
with rules that heavily favored the US government.

00:04:47.824 --> 00:04:50.277
For example, the government 
could introduce evidence 

00:04:50.277 --> 00:04:53.620
without showing it to the detainee first.

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.030
This made Guantánamo Bay prison 
a “legal blackhole”.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:01.841
The original sin is that we created 
an institution outside

00:05:01.841 --> 00:05:05.728
 and designed to be outside the rule of law.

00:05:05.728 --> 00:05:08.171
"No more torture in our name."

00:05:08.171 --> 00:05:11.060
"And shut it down and release everybody."

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:16.380
"Justice for Guantánamo detainees, now a
core celebra among human rights activists."

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:20.420
World leaders, allied governments, and 
US politicians began calling for the closure

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:21.904
of Guantánamo.

00:05:21.904 --> 00:05:25.587
Plus, Bush officials recognized that it was
actually hurting the war on terror.

00:05:25.587 --> 00:05:27.857
Gitmo was also a great recruiting tool.

00:05:27.857 --> 00:05:31.960
There were all these terrorist videos 
about how evil the Americans were

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.335
and we have to fight back because they imprison
people without any legal basis.

00:05:36.335 --> 00:05:37.770
They torture people.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.590
Eventually, the Supreme Court stepped in.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:44.110
In a series of decisions, the Court ruled
that Guantánamo detainees were entitled to

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:45.956
challenge their imprisonment in court.

00:05:47.122 --> 00:05:50.752
It was clear the US would soon have to let
many of these detainees out.

00:05:50.752 --> 00:05:55.349
So, the Bush administration reviewed every
case and set up a transfer process.

00:05:55.349 --> 00:05:57.274
Pretty simple process. Yeah.

00:05:57.274 --> 00:05:58.485
Move them back.

00:05:58.485 --> 00:05:59.880
Mainly to Afghanistan.

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.822
Nobody asked a lot of questions.

00:06:01.822 --> 00:06:06.631
Over a 5 year period, the Bush administration
transferred 532 detainees 

00:06:06.631 --> 00:06:09.510
and only convicted 3 through the military courts.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.365
5 detainees died in the prison.

00:06:11.365 --> 00:06:13.727
Four of them were reportedly suicides.

00:06:13.727 --> 00:06:16.906
That left 242 in Guantánamo when he left office 

00:06:16.906 --> 00:06:19.780
with some hope that the prison would close.

00:06:20.105 --> 00:06:24.904
"Guantánamo will be closed, no later than
one year from now."

00:06:24.904 --> 00:06:29.370
On his second day in office, Barack Obama
signed an executive order to close Guantanamo

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.757
within a year.

00:06:30.757 --> 00:06:33.690
His administration split the detainees into
three groups 

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:36.380
and laid out a plan with more paths out.

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:38.601
The first group included about half the detainees

00:06:38.601 --> 00:06:40.881
who would be transferred either to their home countries.

00:06:40.881 --> 00:06:44.183
Or, for those whose home countries 
were too dangerous or unstable

00:06:44.183 --> 00:06:47.440
the US would negotiate deals 
with other countries to take them.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.370
Or they would be released to the US.

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:52.130
This was the plan for the Uigher men.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:55.594
A second group would be tried 
as criminals in a US federal court.

00:06:55.594 --> 00:06:58.920
Including five men who helped plan 
the 9/11 attacks.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:00.859
But that still left a third group.

00:07:00.859 --> 00:07:05.470
There was a category of people we didn't feel
comfortable transferring

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:10.268
but without sufficient evidentiary basis to
put them on trial.

00:07:10.268 --> 00:07:14.010
This final group of several dozen would be
moved to a super-maximum security prison

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:15.144
in Illinois.

00:07:15.144 --> 00:07:17.141
Then Guantánamo could be closed.

00:07:17.141 --> 00:07:18.684
It was a good enough plan.

00:07:18.684 --> 00:07:20.441
It had risk.

00:07:20.441 --> 00:07:24.170
But keeping Guantánamo open has risks too.

00:07:26.177 --> 00:07:30.330
"Now I believe that any plan to close Guantánamo
that includes bringing these terrorists into

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:33.388
the United States, Mr. President, is a mistake."

00:07:33.388 --> 00:07:38.884
"To do so would be nothing short of an invitation
for Al-Qaeda to operate inside our homeland."

00:07:38.884 --> 00:07:41.858
"In my view, these men are exactly 
where they belong."

00:07:41.858 --> 00:07:47.254
In Congress, Republican politicians fiercely
opposed every aspect of the plan.

00:07:47.254 --> 00:07:51.653
They pushed back against transfers and putting
any detainees on trial in the US.

00:07:51.653 --> 00:07:55.104
"It will make America a more dangerous place."

00:07:55.104 --> 00:07:58.980
"And it will allow them the platform 
to spew their hateful ideology."

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:02.300
They even attacked the plan to move some into
the supermax prison.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:07.780
"You're also putting people who would then
start plotting for their escape from the outside

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:09.370
in America's neighborhoods."

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:14.010
All of a sudden when Obama comes in,
"Oh no, you can't let people go 

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:15.977
because you're letting terrorists out."

00:08:15.977 --> 00:08:19.746
By the spring of '09, this narrative 
was already set:

00:08:19.746 --> 00:08:22.694
"The Obama administration: soft on terrorism”.

00:08:22.694 --> 00:08:26.027
It's fair to ask tough questions.

00:08:26.027 --> 00:08:30.778
What's not reasonable is making it 
impossible for Obama 

00:08:30.778 --> 00:08:33.461
when you didn't ask any hard questions about Bush.

00:08:33.461 --> 00:08:37.754
Ultimately both Republicans and Democrats
passed bills that blocked any Guantánamo

00:08:37.754 --> 00:08:40.199
detainee from coming to the US for any reason.

00:08:40.199 --> 00:08:42.984
Including for trial, imprisonment, or release.

00:08:42.984 --> 00:08:45.422
Then the Obama administration folded.

00:08:45.422 --> 00:08:50.350
They gave up at the first sign that it would
take a lot of political capital to close Gitmo.

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:52.245
But y’all made a big deal of it.

00:08:52.245 --> 00:08:53.379
You better mean it.

00:08:53.379 --> 00:08:56.844
The Obama administration didn't mean it enough.

00:08:56.844 --> 00:09:01.074
The only two options detainees had 
stayed in place but got much harder.

00:09:01.074 --> 00:09:04.263
The military courts were extremely slow 
and ineffective.

00:09:04.263 --> 00:09:06.991
3 convictions had been overturned by 2016.

00:09:06.991 --> 00:09:09.630
And transfers became more complicated.

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:13.500
Many of the detainees were from Yemen but
couldn't be transferred back after conflict

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:15.250
broke out there in 2011.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:18.342
So, the US would have to convince 
other countries to take them.

00:09:18.342 --> 00:09:19.534
That was Dan’s job.

00:09:19.534 --> 00:09:22.796
For years and years, the American government
has said these are the worst of the worst.

00:09:22.796 --> 00:09:24.567
These are terrorist masterminds.

00:09:24.567 --> 00:09:26.954
And now I'm saying, no, no, 
they're actually not.

00:09:26.954 --> 00:09:30.540
We shouldn't have asked other countries 
to take them if we weren't willing to take them.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:33.002
Dan negotiated transfers for 17 Uigher men

00:09:33.002 --> 00:09:35.494
who were not allowed to be released to the US.

00:09:35.494 --> 00:09:39.292
They'd been held in Guantánamo 
for more than a decade.

00:09:39.292 --> 00:09:44.403
Over 8 years, the Obama administration moved
197 detainees out of Guantánamo 

00:09:44.403 --> 00:09:47.084
and convicted 5 through the military courts.

00:09:47.084 --> 00:09:50.691
Four more died inside, three reportedly 
from suicide.

00:09:50.691 --> 00:09:55.540
That left 41 detainees with a new president
who wanted to keep the prison open.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.639
"We're going to keep, as you know, Gitmo,
we're keeping that open."

00:09:59.639 --> 00:10:01.999
"And we're gonna load it up with bad dudes."

00:10:01.999 --> 00:10:07.625
In 2017, Donald Trump took office as a fervent
supporter of keeping Guantánamo open.

00:10:07.625 --> 00:10:12.227
In four years, his administration 
only transferred one detainee.

00:10:12.227 --> 00:10:14.836
In 2021, when President Biden took office

00:10:14.836 --> 00:10:19.249
Moath al-Alwi had been in Guantánamo for 19 years.

00:10:19.249 --> 00:10:23.758
According to his testimony, he went on a series
of hunger strikes to protest his detention.

00:10:23.758 --> 00:10:27.077
And described his life as an 
“endless horror movie”.

00:10:27.077 --> 00:10:32.560
But January 11, 2022, Biden approved Alwi
and four other detainees for transfer.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.558
For Guantánamo to close, the Biden administration
needs to transfer the last remaining detainees.

00:10:37.558 --> 00:10:40.427
And the military courts need to conclude 
trials for the 10 

00:10:40.427 --> 00:10:41.924
who are currently stuck in the system.

00:10:41.924 --> 00:10:46.199
Including the 5 alleged 9/11 plotters, who
have now been on trial for a decade.

00:10:46.199 --> 00:10:49.522
Once you start them outside the rule of law

00:10:49.522 --> 00:10:53.399
bringing them in the rule of law 
is a lot trickier than you think.

00:10:53.399 --> 00:10:56.749
Don't throw out the rulebook in a fit of passion.

00:10:56.749 --> 00:10:58.506
You'll regret it.

00:10:58.506 --> 00:10:59.321
And we did.

